{
  "id": "https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/biological-sex",
  "type": "Concept",
  "prefLabel": {
    "en": "biological sex"
  },
  "definition": {
    "en": "The multidimensional biological characteristics used to describe and classify human bodies, including chromosomes, gonads, hormones, reproductive anatomy, external genitalia, physiology, and secondary sexual characteristics."
  },
  "scopeNote": {
    "en": [
      "These features often align in patterns commonly classified as female or male, but they do not always do so. Variations in sex characteristics, including chromosomal, hormonal, gonadal, or anatomical variations, may fall under the broad category of intersex variations and demonstrate that biological sex cannot always be reduced to a strictly binary classification. Sex is usually assigned at birth, primarily on the basis of external genital characteristics, and may subsequently be recorded as a legal or administrative status. Although sex classification draws on biological features, the criteria through which those features are selected, interpreted, and registered are also shaped by medical, legal, social, and cultural practices. Sex and gender identity are related but analytically distinct concepts. A person’s sex characteristics or assigned sex may not correspond to their gender identity."
    ]
  },
  "notation": [
    "7.10.1"
  ],
  "broader": {
